  the judge is going to allow that to  happen at all yeah i like the fact that  you use the word unique because that's  kind of my question for lisa as to how  unique this is because lisa the crumbly  parents are the only parents being  charged in a mass shooting but there are  some similarities in the highland park  shooting do you think in that case the  shooter's father could be charged  well if we're using the precedent that  we're using right now in this particular  case i would say yes it fits the  criteria  they ignored help for uh his mental  health issues we had april of 2019 he  attempted suicide with machete police  went there they took away his knives and  his daggers then let's fast forward to  september of 2019 where he threatened to  kill his whole family and again no  charges were pressed these are mental  health issues and then we have december  just three months later where father  goes and co-signs and sponsors him to  get a foid card who in their right mind  is going to do that for a son who in the  same year attempted to kill himself and  the family now let's go buy him a gun to  me that does fit the criteria for  involuntary manslaughter yeah and lisa  it's not just what you said it's what's  missing as well in my mind because if  all this happens did you get your son  help in between a social worker uh  psychiatrist anything you're just like  ah it's three months ago let's just buy  you a gun like that is bewildering to me  but we'll see how the cords think of  that one well when we come back a jury  hands down a verdict in colorado as  kevin dean eastman faces a possible  sentence of life in prison for a 2020  double homicide  welcome back to law and crime daily a  colorado man is sentenced a day after  the jury found him guilty of a double  homicide in the trial of kevin dean  eastman  late wednesday night the judge announced  eastman was found guilty on all charges  including two counts of first-degree  murder tampering with a deceased human  body and tampering with physical  evidence for the deaths of his  ex-girlfriend heather frank and her new  love interest local musician stanley  scott sessions in february of 2020.  prosecutors say eastman cut session's  throat after inviting him over to  frank's apartment using her cell phone  eastman and frank were charged in his  murder until it was discovered frank had  also been killed investigators believe  eastman fatally shot frank to silence  the only witness to sessions killing  prosecutors allege eastman burned  session's body before frank's body was  discovered wrapped in plastic hidden on  the rule property of his new job  investigators say he was preparing to  burn her body as well before being  arrested at a nearby gas station  the verdict was handed down seven hours  after the jury began their deliberations  dispensing with the caption as relates  to count number one  murder in the first degree involving  victim stanley scott sessions the  verdict reads we the jury find the  defendant kevin dean eastman guilty  of murder in the first degree after  deliberation  as it relates to count number two  murder in the first degree involving  victim heather frank  the verdict reads as follows  we the jury find the defendant kevin  dean eastman  guilty of murder in the first degree  after deliberation  the jury further found that this was an  act of domestic violence we the jury  find the defendant kevin dean eastman  guilty of tampering with a deceased  human body we the jury find the  defendant kevin dean eastman  guilty of tampering with physical  evidence  on thursday eastman learned his sentence  after being convicted of murder  what i will say  mr eastman is that you are going to  spend the rest of your  natural life in prison  based on your  vicious acts of violence  jealousy and control  and the taking of two precious lives  and thereafter disposing of  their deceased bodies in such a  disrespectful manner that's why you're  going to spend the rest  of your life  in prison the court is going to sentence  you  to  a life sentence in the colorado  department of corrections  without the possibility of  pearl  all right so lisa clearly the jury  didn't buy that bonnell and frank were  in on these murders why do you think  that was  because simply was a ludicrous defense  what did bonnell what skin in the game  did he have to  help bury the bodies get rid of the  bodies and then  say later on that he also killed frank  because he panicked there's no motive  for him to do this it was a hail mary  pass it didn't work they didn't buy it i  think anybody that was watching the  trial thought oh wow that's a real a  really far stretch for him to blame his  former co-worker  for the whole kitten caboodle for  killing his wife and for killing  sessions it didn't make sense at all  what i will say this is a great teaching  moment in terms of what's the difference  between a doubt and a reasonable doubt i  don't think the jury found this to be a  reasonable doubt anything can be a doubt  and it wasn't enough to carry the  defense's case well terry why is the  sentencing being done so soon i mean  typically this happens weeks sometimes  months afterwards and did the time uh  with the time life without parole  fit the crime in this double homicide  it definitely fit the crime brian and in  colorado if you are convicted of a crime  of violence that carries a mandatory  prison sentence meaning that the judge  has no choice but to sentence you to  prison so the judge can increase the  sentence but he can't reduce it but here  he was convicted of first degree murder  in colorado first degree murder is life  life is life so i think the judge just  decided look i don't think i'm going to  change my mind here nothing is going to  change the decision  and he wanted to get it over now  obviously there could be you know victim  impact statements but the judge had  already made up his mind that he was  going to do life life without parole and  so that's exactly what he did he didn't  think there was a reason to hold it over  makes sense why there was no delay in  that and just kind of move right forward  well lisa terry thank you very much and  thank you for joining us here on law and  crime daily we'll see you next time as  we discuss justice in america  [Music]  you
 

